"An LSM tree diagram explains buffered writes, immutable sorted runs, and compaction under a specified storage-engine strategy.\n\nUse when: Explain write amplification and read paths qualitatively. Review how compaction reorganizes sorted storage files.\n\nAvoid when: The task requires an exact observed disk layout without engine evidence.\n\nVisual grammar: Regions distinguish memory buffers, write-ahead logging, and disk levels. File cards show sorted key ranges rather than invented sizes. Arrows separately encode flush, compaction, and lookup paths.\n\nBuild: Name the engine and compaction strategy being illustrated. Draw active and immutable memtables with their persistence path. Place sorted files in levels and label their key ranges. Route compaction arrows through dedicated side corridors away from range labels.\n\nCheck: Overlap rules match the selected level and strategy. Newest-value resolution and tombstones are represented correctly. Schematic ranges and observed file metrics are explicitly distinguished. Flush and lookup arrows remain separate from compaction labels.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming every LSM engine uses the same level-overlap rules. Treating tombstones as immediate physical deletion.\n\nExample: A RocksDB-style leveled view shows a memtable flush into overlapping level-zero files before compaction into sorted lower-level ranges.\n\nAlternatives: B-tree index diagram, Storage partition diagram."
